Nancy opines:

>you can kill animals (or people, for that matter)
>so long as it's for the government's purposes and not for your
>own.

I became aware of another example of this today.  A close
friend of mine just discovered that her temporary federal
civil service job exists for the following reasons...

1) The Air Force SP's want to shoot somebody or something.

2) It is hard to justify shooting people in peacetime.

3) There are coyotes on the air base and they are excellent
targets (being rather smarter than people anyway when it comes
to evading hunters).

4) The SP's shot a few coyotes out of the New York State
season on the animals, and want to do so again.

5) To keep the base environmental people out of trouble
with the state environmental people, somebody has to
count the coyotes to prove that there are enough of them
to present a possible hazard to aircraft and personnel.

I'm not outraged or anything (coyotes are a big nuisance
and nowhere near endangered, in fact the legislature
passed an open season [year around] on them but the
governor refused to sign it).  It just strikes me as
typical that the boys with their toys need to spend
lots of our money to keep other boys (also on our
payroll) off their backs when they break the rules.
