Date: Wed, 29 Dec 1993 21:11:13 -0800 (PST)
From: joh@netcom.com (John Oh)
Subject: Extropially Correct Welfare

Harry Shapiro mentions that intelligence alone does not determine ability to
work well and make money.

Definitely.  I realize there are other important factors.  An employee's 
attitude is critical.  I'm looking to hire new employees right now, and I'm 
carefully thinking about these other factors.  However, intelligence is an
important prerequisite, and if you try to select the top fraction of a percent
of children in intelligence, I'm sure you'll have better luck than choosing
randomly.  Of course, you can try to assess other factors as well during the
selection process, to whatever extent that will be possible at such a young 
age.  Also, you can try to inculcate work ethics into the children as part o
the education.

Also, there are different ways that the employees can be valuable.  Even if
a particular person is not well suited to be a manager for various reasons
they still may do well as a researcher.  

