>From what I know, these things are not mutually exclusive, but having
superior physical features distracts people from purely intellectual
pursuits, and it is often lack of success with the opposite sex, etc.
that gives kids time to increase their intelligence via reading.
The percentage of physically handicapped/short/weak people among great
historical figures seems higher than average, even if those qualities
were handicapping their advance.
        
>  Also, I think that most parents would definitely want their children to
>substantially above average in intelligence, although perhaps not too much 
>above average.
 
   I read somewhere ("Magical Child" by Pierce?) that there's "an optimal
IQ range" - about 115 to 135.  People with these IQs are already above the
crowd, but not too much - their interests are still the same, they chase
the same things as others, but do it better, which makes them natural
leaders with everything that follows: social recognition, wealth, etc.
If your IQ is off the chart (or your features are beyond the IQ measurement),
you are likely to pursue different things; people don't understand (or pay)
you, you don't want to lead them, etc. - you're a natural outcast.
 
  Fortunately for the intelligence increase, it's quite enough to have
people want to be just a bit higher than average - or even not to fall
into the last 10%.   Even with purely 'catch-up' intelligence upgrades
people would be shooting at the moving target of the increasing average IQs,
and the process would quickly escalate as far as the technology may allow.
 
  An IQ race...
 
  I would expect though that the first 'self-enhancement' race won't be
in intelligence.  Building more flesh seems both technologically easier
and more widely appealing.
  I wonder where the body-builders will stop when they get access to
cheap sources of unlimited muscle mass.
 
