Date: Thu, 30 Dec 1993 02:27:08 -0230
From: Bill Garland <bill@kean.ucs.mun.ca>
Subject: Teach a Man to Fish...NOT!

As I have been busy reading FUTD and not keeping up with my mail,
it was quite fun today trying to catch up with 315 new messages,
so far all of them from Extropians. Many came in since this morning,
too, and I still have 79 messages to go.

At least two of these 400 or so msgs that I skimmed today (several
dozen came in while reading the first few hundred- hey, you all 
know about this phenomenum !) had mention of the "Teach a man to 
fish" meme. 

[Please note, my COD (Concise Oxford Dictionary) does not define meme nor
memetics, so my use of the word is from how I interpret others' uses of the
word. I have not yet had time to do all my Extropian homework, such as send
for all back issues and read all the books on the list that I hadn't read
before joining the List (May 93). Anyway... 

I wish to discourage the use of this meme henceforth, as I have found
it wanting. You see, I escaped from Toronto about fifteen or twenty
years ago, after some very enlightening experiences, but a very
unfortunate experience as a victim of the WOSD. I returned to my
native Newfoundland, and became a fisherman. It is there on my
BlackNet record...check the year 1977, where all of my income
was from fishing...

Teach a man to fish, indeed! It was, in fact, my success at fishing
that landed me (pun intended) my current job. In the pre-job-interview
-that-was-really-the-actual-job-interview, I first explained to the
power-that-was how much I knew about the technical stuff I wanted
to work with, and showed him my drawings of circuits I had built
and used in a laboratory. He was not overly impressed, but at least
knew I was worth a few more minutes of his time. I also explained
to him the nature of my encounter with the drug warriors and my
subsequent loss of scholarship, job, money, self-esteem, etc. He
sympathised a little bit, and then said that many people get knocked
down  by similar encounters, and not to worry about it. He said it
was much more important to show what I had done since then in 
picking myself up off the ground and becoming a success in spite
of it all. Good man. (He was eventually fired by the establishment
for being too businesslike in an academic environment...but that's
another story.)

So then I told him about my experience fishing. I had designed and
built my own squid-jigger, and made a living jigging squid that 
summer. I was unemployed, broke, and depressed for about a year,
when I said fuck this, and got out of the big city and went back
home. My father, who all his life wanted to be a fisherman, but 
who could never escape the paper mills whereby he made his living,
had retired and purchased a couple of boats to do just that, i.e.
to go fishing. He was too old and worn out to pull it off, but 
I was young and healthy and the fishery was prosperous. Neither
one of us knew enough about fishing to make a go of it, until the
squid struck in. Well, I was out watching for a while, and I went
back to the shed and made Version 1 of my squid jigger. It was not
industrial strength. I used plastic margarine tubs as the sheaves,
and built it all from scrap wood and metal lying about. 

It lasted about a day and a half, but I made thirty or forty bucks
from it, and I earned the respect of some of the "bona fide" 
fishermen. They didn't acutally laugh in my face when the saw V1,
but I bet they did have a good chuckle behind my back. However, one
of them showed me _his_ rig, which was about version 4 or 5 for him,
and told me about the key ingredient to making an industrial
strength squid jigger. It was the piston-rod from an old car engine.
You use a pair of them to mount two nailed-together rope-coil thingys
with a metal axle down the middle, and there was no way around using
proper Japanese sheaves. These large plastic guides cost twenty
bucks each and were in short supply that year.

So it was off to the dead-car yards to find the piston rods.
I found them, and built Version Two. Squido! My forty bucks from
version one were re-invested into V2, and I was making money.
I made $4000 in eight weeks. Not big money by today's standards,
but for someone who had been broke for a year, it was paradise.
I paid cash for everything, including my wife's engagement ring.
In fact, I made enough money to get my unemployment "stamps",
which would have entitled me to free government money for the
entire winter...except that I got a job and went to work.

Well, anyway, the fishery was prosperous for me and for several
thousand others that year and the next and so on. For about a
decade. We had recently declared a two-hundred mile limit for
the coastal boundary, and were busy making every man jack into
a fisherman. There were new fish plants going up everywhere.
A bumper crop of trawlers were purchased.

It was and still is, to a certain extent, considered a natural
birthright of all Newfoundlanders to go fishing. That is where
our society came from, and that was to be what sustained us
forever. Renewable resource, and all that.

So now the party is over. There are no more fish. Teach a man
to fish is fine, as long as it is only one man. Teach a society
to fish, and what do you get? The current appellation is
"Moratorium". All fishing has been prohibited for the past two
years. The breeding stock are gone. It takes about seven years
for cod to grow to the breeding stage, and catch failures have
been the only story of the past few years. There are many
theories as to why the fish are gone. Too many foreigners
fishing outside the 200 mile limit. Too many seals eating the
cod (they don't eat turnips). Too many draggers. Destruction
of the habitat by the draggers. The water is too cold. They
fish during spawning season They are too efficient with their
trawls, sonar, fish-finders, loran, nylon nets, small mesh
sizes, and so on. Most often we blame the foreigners and the
seals, but the real reason, I suspect, is the massive overfishing
by everyone everywhere all year long. Draggers got to meet their
quotas so they sail every ten days. A friend of mine was washed
overboard while chipping ice off the superstructure of his 
dragger. They should never have been out in that weather.

And so it goes. Many alarmists see this as the knee of the
curve, where the oceans will die because of overfishing.
The squid were back in 1978, but they haven't been back since.
The Japanese fished out their own waters decades ago, and came
here with lots of money they earned by selling us cars, and now
our squid are gone. They buy capelin, which are the feedstocks
for the cod. They only take female capelin, for the roe,
because it is a delicacy. The males are sent on to the meal
plant, to become cat food or fertilizer.

Well, I suppose I could rave on some more, but I've only just
got into Book Two of FUTD, and still have 85 extropian mail
messages to read...and it's back to work in the morning.

Teach a man to fish, and we'll all be eating turnips forever.

	Bill Garland,
		whose .sig tells him to fish or cut bait

