and who choose the latter, are not demonstrating lack of
intelligence by choosing the less lucrative trade.

I think that goes back to my point about Statist toadies.

There is an excellent report from the National Bureau of
Economic Research entitled _Allocation of Talent:
Implications for Growth_.  NBER purports to be a bunch
of purely scientific economists without any axe to grind,
and who am I to say they are not.  The study I cite correlates
real GNP growth rates in various countries with the rates
of engineering and law school grad production.  More engineers
than lawyers -> growth.  More lawyers than engineers -> slower
growth or even shrinkage.  Surprise!  NBER's researchers
say that engineers and lawyers aren't the only careers
which have these effects, but that they are prototypical.
Engineers are considered by the researchers to be
'wealth producers' whereas lawyers are considered to be
'wealth redistributors'.  Obviously the former increase
the net wealth in the society as a whole, whereas the latter
merely move it around.  Societies (like our own) which reward
wealth redistribution more richly than wealth creation get,
you're gonna find this a real surprise, LESS WEALTH!

I subdivide wealth redistributors into those who seduce
people into giving their wealth away, and those who take
it with a gun.  The latter are my enemy and although
Tim May may modestly disclaim the title of warrior,
I claim it with pride, and will fight my enemy with
every moral means available (it is up to the 'fraidy-cats'
to figure out what means I consider moral and whether my
intended use of such means frightens them).

