>nature, but because alternative living arrangements, communities,
>etc., are an intrinisically interesting topic).

Whenever I mention cohousing on this list, I get lots of requests for more
information.  Unfortunately, they're all vague, like the above.  But if I
wrote down everything anybody might find interesting, it would be 10,000
words long, and I don't have the time.

Fortunately, there are some specific questions to be answered:

>I've read about these cohousing groups in Denmark and elsewhere, and
>about one getting started on Whidby Island, near Seattle. Sounds
>interesting, though I'd just as soon have my own house, 

Everybody gets their own house, or apartment.  It's like a condominium
development that way.  There are also common facilities like roads, lawns,
dining hall, parking lots, sewage treatment plant, etc.  

>with my own
>laws,

There are extra laws, which hopefully can be enforced by social pressure
rather than force.  Unfortunately we don't get to disallow government laws
we don't like.  If we could, it would be a PPL.

>my own budget, and so on.

Lots of people are sinking their entire net worth into this scheme.  It's scary.

>Still, various tech folks I know have tried variants of communal
>living---usually they're a bunch of hacker bachelor slobs, so the
>results are less than overwhelmingly positive. And the occasional
>shared housing by some Extropians is another step. Someday I would not
>be surprised to see more serious arrangements.

I lived for seven years in a house with four to eight mostly hacker mostly
unmarried mostly slobs, and it's still going twelve years after we started
it.  I think that qualifies as a success.  I know of two or three other
places with similar characteristics.

>Do the cohousing groups
>have time to screen newcomers adequately as vacancies appear? 

Yes.  It takes a few weeks.  It's actually a lot less intense than my 
college fraternity.  As far as I know we've never actually rejected anyone,
because we'll take anyone who wants to put up with us.  Seems to work.

>Will
>they pick up the mortgage payments on empty units? 

A topic of current debate, I think.

>Will the departed
>ones have to sell to approved buyers?

If you can't sell to an approved buyer after six months of trying, you can
sell to anyone.  But there's a long waiting list, so the group would have
to become undesirable for this to be a problem.

I have to drop off the list for a while to write a paper, but I will be
glad to discuss cohousing if there's more interest.  If you want to discuss
it, send the message to both me and the list.

Ta-ta for now,


