Notes from my renting spree at the range, yesterday:  1) Unless you have
very small hands, the compact Glocks (19 and 23) are too small to grip
comfortably; my pinky grabs air, and my ring finger sometimes slips from
the kick.  2) The .40 caliber does _not_ have appreciably more kick than
the 9mm.  3) As I think others have previously noted, the Glock is
rediculously easy to disassemble and clean, as semi-autos go.  4) The
fixed-sight seems to be pretty accurate (non of the four models I tried
were noticibly off at 10 yards--either that or _I_ was consistently off),
so there appears to be no good reason to get the 1-axis adjustable sight;
word is it gets knocked off easily anyway.

I ended up deciding on a fixed-sight model 22, the full-size .40.  Those
of you recently calling around in the south Bay area for Glocks have no
doubt noticed that they are out of stock at most local stores, except for
a few small places with the audacity to charge > $100 over going market.
The best price I could find today (no, I'm not patient enough to wait for
a sale, if there ever is a sale on such a hot item) was $459 at the
National Shooting Club in Santa Clara, and surprisingly, they had several
in stock.

I suggest shooting the guns you are interested in--not just handling and
hearing about them--before making a final purchase decision.  This extends
to well-meaning gifts for your esso, male or female; get them to _try_ it
and be sure they _like_ it before buying it for them; otherwise they may
stay intimidated and/or unpracticed, thereby short-circuiting your good
intentions for their safety.  In the south Bay, Target Masters in Milpitas
and the National Shooting Club in Santa Clara have quite a few rentables.

Profuse thanks to those of you who have provided gun info on the list and
in subsequent private mail.  

Feeling a little more empowered, and hopefully living longer because of it,

